WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:08.201
You frequently hear about global hedge funds managing billions of dollars and moving financial markets.

00:00:08.151 --> 00:00:13.529
But what are they, and how do they differ from retail mutual funds?

00:00:13.529 --> 00:00:24.141
A Hedge Fund is an alternative, private pooled investment vehicle designed for institutional investors and ultra-high-net-worth individuals.

00:00:24.141 --> 00:00:34.089
In India, hedge funds operate under Category III Alternative Investment Funds, or AIFs, regulated by SEBI.

00:00:34.089 --> 00:00:51.602
Unlike traditional mutual funds—which primarily buy assets expecting them to rise—hedge funds employ sophisticated, flexible, and aggressive trading strategies designed to generate positive returns in both rising and falling markets.

00:00:51.602 --> 00:00:57.279
Hedge fund managers utilize advanced financial tools including:

00:00:57.279 --> 00:01:07.904
1. Long and Short positions: buying undervalued companies while short-selling overvalued firms to profit when their prices drop.

00:01:07.904 --> 00:01:15.352
2. Financial Leverage: borrowing capital to magnify trading positions and return potential.

00:01:15.352 --> 00:01:27.656
3. Derivatives & Arbitrage: using complex options, futures, and interest rate swaps to hedge risks or exploit temporary price inefficiencies.

00:01:27.656 --> 00:01:38.307
Because of their complexity and risk profile, hedge funds require high minimum investments—typically ₹1 Crore or more in India.

00:01:38.307 --> 00:01:52.852
They also charge higher fees, traditionally structured as a '2 and 20' model: a 2% annual management fee plus a 20% performance fee on profits earned above a specified hurdle rate.

00:01:52.852 --> 00:01:59.375
A common misconception is that the word 'hedge' means the fund is low risk or safe.

00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:10.729
In truth, the aggressive use of leverage and short positions can lead to sharp volatility and substantial losses if market hypotheses fail.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:25.521
Hedge funds have lock-in periods, high expense structures, and limited liquidity, making them unsuitable for everyday retail investors who are far better served by low-cost index funds.

00:02:25.521 --> 00:02:37.174
Hedge funds are specialized, high-minimum private funds using complex strategies and leverage to pursue absolute returns across all market conditions.
